<section id="s1" class="part">
<h2>Landed since your last approval</h2>
<p>You read items 28 and 29 at 06:42 UTC on 7 November 2026, so they are not on the page.</p>
<p>Two items, 30 and 31, changes to the coordinator skill that you have not read. Each shows its change against the text before it.</p>
<p>In the item, the words that changed are underlined, and the words that went are struck through. To reverse a change, send a comment to Claude on the item, or say so in chat. The skill writer then restores the old words.</p>

<section id="l30" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 30 &middot; batch.md, &ldquo;The go&rdquo;</span>What a batch record takes</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text, against the text before the change; underlined, the words that came in. No words of that text went. In the skill's words, &ldquo;you&rdquo; is the coordinator.</span>
<p class="where-in">references/batch.md, &ldquo;The go&rdquo;, a new bullet, line 38</p>
<ul><li><span class="add">Put into a batch record every piece of work on PLAN&rsquo;s road that moves the project forward and is well defined: its decisions are taken. A new deliverable still waits for its go (<code>SKILL.md</code>, &ldquo;The curator&rsquo;s role and decisions&rdquo;). A piece that is not well defined waits. A well-defined piece waits only if it would put the batch&rsquo;s merge at risk. Two rungs put it at risk if the gather cannot merge their edits of one file or one declaration cleanly. A pair that merges cleanly but changes one behaviour two ways, or where one needs the other&rsquo;s result, goes in one batch only if one lands only with the other (the manifest&rsquo;s <code>landsOnlyWith</code>).</span></li></ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>A new rule.</strong> The skill said when a batch is launched, but not which work its record takes. Between 05:20 and 05:30 UTC on 7 November, when the two code-generator rungs, <code>asif</code> and the trait table, joined climb batch 27, you stated the rule in chat: &ldquo;if they were well defined and not in conflict, why not include all work that moves us forward in a batch? But if there would be a risk to merge, I&rsquo;d delay it to deconflict a batch landing.&rdquo;</p>
<p>The text now says which work a batch takes and what puts the merge at risk. A batch record takes every piece of work on the plan&rsquo;s road that moves the project forward and is well defined, which means that its decisions are taken. A new deliverable still waits for your go, and a piece that is not well defined waits. A well-defined piece waits only if it would put the merge at risk. Two rungs put the merge at risk when the gather cannot cleanly merge their edits of one file or one declaration. Two rungs that merge cleanly but change one behaviour in two ways, or where one needs the other&rsquo;s result, go in one batch only if the manifest ties one to land only with the other.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l31" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 31 &middot; batch.md, &ldquo;While it runs&rdquo;, and SKILL.md, &ldquo;The record: what goes where&rdquo;</span>While a batch runs, the main tree stays open until every rung has started</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text, against the text before the change; struck through, the words that went; underlined, the words that came in. In the skill's words, &ldquo;you&rdquo; is the coordinator.</span>
<p class="where-in">references/batch.md, &ldquo;While it runs&rdquo;, the second bullet, line 78</p>
<ul><li><span class="cut">From the launch until the run ends, by landing, by stopping or by holding its push for the curator, edit no tracked file in the main tree, the boot note included. The</span> <span class="add">The rungs, their skeptics and their judges work in their own worktrees, and do not read the main tree. The gather and the stages after it work in the main tree, and the</span> gather needs <code>git status --porcelain</code> there to print nothing. <span class="add">Until the run&rsquo;s journal holds a <code>started</code> line labelled <code>rung:&lt;id&gt;</code> for every rung of the batch, you and each worker that you start in the main tree, the skill writer included, may commit there the files that no rung and no stage writes, such as the skills, the boot note and the held list. Commit each edit by named paths, and push it at once. Leave no uncommitted change and no untracked file there. After that last <code>started</code> line, start no such task. From then until the batch&rsquo;s push, change nothing in the main tree. Keep held points and boot-note changes in <code>&lt;scratchpad&gt;/batch&lt;N&gt;/held-in-batch.md</code> meanwhile, and write them into the held list and the boot note after the push. If the commit stage holds its push for the curator, take only the landing&rsquo;s steps 1 to 3 there, on the local commits, until you push the batch after the curator&rsquo;s go. At the landing, name on the review list, as yours, each commit under <code>.claude/</code> that you or the skill writer made after the base, since the commit stage lists it as the batch&rsquo;s. Give their hashes to the reviewer of &ldquo;The landing&rdquo;, step 3.</span> Keep <code>origin/main</code> at or behind the main tree&rsquo;s head, or git refuses the commit stage&rsquo;s push. So push <code>main</code> from no other tree. From the main tree, push nothing that the batch committed: the commit stage pushes it.</li></ul>
<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;The record: what goes where&rdquo;, the bullet on committing, line 234, its last sentence</p>
<ul><li>Commit only the paths that you name. Commit a tracked file with <code>git commit -- &lt;paths&gt;</code>, which is the same as <code>git commit --only &lt;paths&gt;</code>. For a new file, first run <code>git add -- &lt;paths&gt;</code>, with the paths named, then commit it in the same way. Never use <code>git add -A</code>, <code>git add .</code> or <code>git commit -a</code>, and never commit without paths: each takes changes that are not yours. The main tree is the checkout at <code>/home/user/fortress</code>, not a worktree, and a batch&rsquo;s gather stages its files there. While a batch runs, <span class="cut">edit nothing tracked there (<code>references/batch.md</code>, &ldquo;While it runs&rdquo;).</span> <span class="add">change the main tree only as <code>references/batch.md</code>, &ldquo;While it runs&rdquo;, allows.</span></li></ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>A rule&rsquo;s meaning changed.</strong> The old text forbade any edit of a tracked file in the main tree, the boot note included, from a batch&rsquo;s launch to its landing. But the batch script shows that the rungs, their skeptics and their judges work in their own worktrees, and that only the gather and the stages after it work in the main tree. You said in chat between 07:10 and 07:20 UTC on 7 November that the skills were edited during batch runs before, and that only the gather is sensitive.</p>
<p>The text now lets the coordinator and the skill writer commit the skills, the boot note and the held list in the main tree until every rung&rsquo;s worker has started. The same holds for any other worker that the coordinator starts there, and for any file that no rung and no stage writes. Each edit is committed by its named paths and pushed at once, and nothing is left uncommitted. From the start of the last rung&rsquo;s worker until the batch&rsquo;s push, the main tree is frozen. Held points and changes to the boot note wait meanwhile in a scratch file of the batch, and go into the held list and the boot note after the push. If the commit stage holds the push for you, the coordinator takes only the landing&rsquo;s first three steps until it pushes the batch at your go. At the landing, the coordinator names its own skill commits on the review list, since the commit stage lists them as the batch&rsquo;s, and gives their hashes to the landing&rsquo;s reviewer. The sentence in <code>SKILL.md</code> now points to this bullet in place of the old ban.</p></div>
</section>

